First Author: Tian
Title: Machine learning model predicts airway stenosis requiring clinical intervention in patients after lung transplantation: a retrospective case-controlled study
Source number: 51  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective single-center study developed machine-learning models to predict airway stenosis (AS) requiring clinical intervention after lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 381 lung transplant recipients were included, and 40 patients (10.5%) developed AS requiring intervention; (2) multivariate logistic regression identified male sex, pulmonary arterial hypertension, and lower postoperative 6-minute walking test distance as independent predictors; (3) 56 ML models were built from 7 feature-selection methods and 8 algorithms; (4) the best model was a random forest with determination-coefficient feature selection; and (5) the optimal ML model outperformed conventional logistic regression, achieving AUC 0.760 (95% CI 0.666–0.864) and Brier score 0.085 (95% CI 0.058–0.117). Figures 1–5 and Tables 1–4 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

